    "available_at": "2026-11-09T20:29:51.756722+00:00",
    "body": "A truck is pictured at a Barrick mine in Nevada. Credit: Barrick Mining via Facebook.\nPosted By: FREDERIC TOMESCO November 9, 2026\nBarrick Mining (TSX: ABX; NYSE: B) said it plans to file for an initial public offering of its North American gold business this month as it reported better-than-expected third-quarter results. The stock rose.\nCompletion of the IPO could take place in the fourth quarter or early 2027, Barrick said Monday . Called North American Barrick, the proposed company would hold interests in Nevada Gold Mines’ Carlin, Cortez and Turquoise Ridge operations, along with Pueblo Viejo in the Dominican Republic.\n“All eyes are on the IPO,” Jefferies mining analyst Fahad Tariq said Monday in a note. He called the offering “the next major catalyst” for Barrick shares.\nThe offering follows an August agreement with Newmont (NYSE, ASX: NEM) to contribute Barrick’s Fourmile project and Newmont’s Mike and Fiberline properties to their Nevada Gold Mines joint venture. Newmont will pay Barrick about $2 billion (C$2.8 billion), with the contributions expected this quarter. The agreement secured Newmont’s consent to the IPO while preserving the joint venture’s ownership split of 61.5% for Barrick and 38.5% for Newmont.\nMark Hill, Barrick’s CEO, will also lead the new company. Completion remains subject to market conditions, approvals and securities registration.\nBarrick’s Securities and Exchange Commission (SEC) filing will include an S-1 form and accompanying technical reports, Hill said. The speed of the SEC feedback will determine when the IPO takes place, he added.\n“We’re still trying to get this done this year but it may slide into January,” the CEO told analysts on a conference call.\nBarrick shares rose 4.4% to C$59.73 Monday afternoon in Toronto, valuing the company at about C$97 billion ($69 billion). The stock has traded between C$45.28 and C$74 in the past year.\nRising output\nBarrick outlined the timetable in its third-quarter management discussion and analysis, alongside results showing higher costs but unchanged annual production guidance. Gold output reached 816,000 oz., up 3% from the previous quarter and down 2% year-over-year on a reported basis.\nNet income rose 1% from a year earlier to $1.3 billion, while revenue jumped 22% to $5.1 billion, Barrick said. Adjusted earnings of 82¢ a share topped the 79¢ consensus cited by TD Securities in its note.\n“All else equal, we are encouraged by strong third-quarter operational performance which highlighted strong cost control despite diesel/consumable cost pressures, reiterated guidance and advancement of its North American IPO towards completion in Q4/26 or early 2027,” National Bank Financial mining analyst Shane Nagle said in a note.\n“The balance sheet remains in a strong position with shareholder returns supported further by proceeds from the partial Fourmile sale and North American IPO.”\nHigher costs\nGold all-in sustaining costs climbed 24% from a year earlier to $1,911 per oz., reflecting lower grades, increased diesel prices, gold-price-linked royalties and higher sustaining capital. The measure also increased from $1,866 per oz. in the second quarter, despite cash costs declining sequentially.\nBarrick reaffirmed its 2026 gold production forecast of 2.9 million to 3.25 million oz. and gold all-in sustaining cost guidance of $1,760–$1,950 per oz. Fourth-quarter gold production should be slightly higher than the third quarter, it said.\nCopper costs face a potential overrun. Barrick maintained production guidance of 190,000–220,000 tonnes, but warned in its management discussion that copper cost measures could finish at or slightly above the upper range of guidance because of diesel costs and local currency appreciation. The published copper all-in sustaining cost range remains $3.45–$3.75 per pound.\n“We’ll be around the midpoint or slightly above the guidance for this year,” Hill said. He advised analysts not to extrapolate when calculating estimates for 2027 costs.\nIn North America, “costs have been controlled quite well,” Hill added. “The things that are driving [the] rest of the world are obviously things that are outside of our control, which is fuel price, royalties and foreign exchange.” All of Barrick’s copper operations posted higher-than-expected costs in the latest period, Scotiabank mining analyst Tanya Jakusconek wrote in a note. Copper output of 50,000 tonnes missed her forecast by about 10%, while copper all-in sustaining costs of $4.52 per lb. were 26% above her estimate.\nMali recovery\nHigher metal prices helped offset operating pressures. Barrick generated attributable free cash flow of $640 million and returned $1.04 billion to shareholders through dividends and buybacks. It finished September with about $909 million in net cash.\nOperationally, the recovery at Loulo-Gounkoto in Mali continued ahead of schedule, with production rising 16% from the previous quarter. Goldrush’s ramp-up and record roaster throughput at Carlin also supported output. However, weather and power disruptions have left Veladero, Porgera and North Mara around or below their annual production ranges.\nAt Lumwana in Zambia, an ongoing expansion remains on budget and scheduled for first copper at the end of the first quarter of 2028. Barrick expects the project to roughly double annual production. All major long-lead equipment has been procured, and construction of the tailings storage expansion is expected to start this quarter.\nReko Diq in Pakistan remains under review because of security, financing, scope and timeline considerations. Barrick’s previously reduced annual capital budget of $3.8 billion–$4.2 billion reflects lower spending. The company continues to review the project’s scope, financing, capital requirements and timetable, leaving its eventual cost and development schedule unresolved. Investors should expect news on Reko Diq by the end of June, Barrick executives said Monday.\n“We’re doing a full review of where we think we should be going with the project. That’s multiple workstreams we’re looking at,” Barrick International CEO Seb Bock said on the call. “By the middle of next year, we’d be able to give you a much clearer picture around that project and and how we see that going forward.”\nPrint\nTAG: copper production gold gold mining gold production Initial public offering Nevada\nCOMMODITY: Copper Gold\nREGION: Africa Canada United States\nCOMPANY: Barrick Mining Newmont\nRepublish this article",
